\section*{Power-free values of irreducible polynomials of degrees four to eight}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 020; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that irreducible quartics without a fixed square divisor have the predicted squarefree density; degrees four through eight satisfy the corresponding $(d-2)$-power-free density and known higher-degree results extend it.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For an irreducible $f\in\mathbb Z[x]$ of degree $d\ge4$, put $k=d-2$ and $\rho_f(p^k)=|\{a\bmod p^k:f(a)\equiv0\pmod{p^k}\}|$. Assume no prime has its $k$th power dividing every value of $f$, equivalently $\rho_f(p^k)<p^k$ for every prime. The claimed asymptotic is
\[
 |\{1\le n\le X:f(n)\text{ is }k\text{-free}\}|
 =c_{f,k}X+o_f(X),\qquad
 c_{f,k}=\prod_p(1-\rho_f(p^k)/p^k)>0.
\]
The theorem proves the range $4\le d\le8$, and its cited all-degree corollary extends the assertion to all $d\ge4$. No primitivity or leading-coefficient sign requirement is imposed; the quartic case is squarefreeness.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 020; 2026-09-24), the finite-prime sieve leaves a tail involving primes larger than the input interval. Number-field factorization and paired boxes are cut by adaptive auxiliary-prime determinants, with explicit geometry in low degrees controlling that tail before the cutoff is removed.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The Euler-product density concerns the stated irreducible polynomials and the absence of a fixed prime-power divisor. The source also extends its initial degree range by combining other results. Complete coverage of this exact record is not established by this catalogue, so it remains partial.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 020; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: irreducible quartics without a fixed square divisor have the predicted squarefree density; degrees four through eight satisfy the corresponding $(d-2)$-power-free density and known higher-degree results extend it.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{PowerFreeValues.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.QuarticPowerFree.allDegrees}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

